      "motivation": "The future of energy storage for renewable wind and solar power needs technologies that are safe, low-cost, resource-abundant, and long-lived. Solid-state sodium-ion batteries are attractive because sodium is more abundant than lithium and solid electrolytes can improve safety, and tunable sulfide-chloride electrolytes are a promising route to stable Na-ion conduction [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4410507001). However, the field still lacks a design rule that stabilizes electrode-electrolyte interfaces over long lifetimes, even though interface control is recognized as a central challenge [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W4200315733). Current research often improves isolated elements, such as computational optimization of flow-battery electrodes [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W4242739072), alternative molecular energy carriers such as formate [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W4234705137), or recycling of lithium-ion electrode materials [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4384282629), but these advances do not directly solve coupled ion and electron transport instability at solid-state sodium interfaces. The proposed idea transfers material-and-energy-balance closure from chemical-engineering flow chemistry [Author et al., 2019](https://openalex.org/W3011563721) to battery interface design: the interface is treated as a flow reactor in which Na+ flux, electronic leakage, side-reaction consumption, heat, and mechanical strain must be balanced. The testable claim is that a graded sulfide-chloride interphase designed to close this local balance will causally suppress resistance growth and dendrite nucleation relative to uniform or randomized interphases of the same average composition.",
