      "title": "Self-Compensating Sulfide–Chloride Interphases Controlled by a Phase-Field Flux-Balance Criterion for Stable Solid-State Sodium Energy Storage",
      "motivation": "The future of energy storage requires safe, low-cost, resource-abundant systems that can absorb intermittent renewable electricity. Sodium-based solid-state batteries are attractive because sodium reduces reliance on lithium, and sulfide-chloride solid electrolytes can combine high ionic conductivity with tunable stability [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4410507001). However, the field still lacks a coupled design rule for interfaces, where Na+ transport, electronic leakage, side reactions, and chemomechanical stress jointly determine lifetime. Component-level optimization alone is insufficient, as shown by redox-flow electrode design [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W4242739072) and recycling-oriented sorting of lithium-ion electrode materials [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4384282629). The unresolved bottleneck is a stable, self-regulating electrode-electrolyte interface. We therefore adapt phase-field multiphysics modeling from chemical engineering, which couples thermodynamics, kinetics, and electrochemistry through evolving fields [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4398203391), and rate-limiting electrochemical process analysis [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4205252019), to create and test a self-compensating sulfide-chloride interphase.",
